      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Heart diseases requiring glycoside therapy are often associated with coronary artery disease. This study evaluated quantitatively the effect of an intravenous bolus injection of digoxin (0.8 mg) on diameters of epicardial coronary arteries in 11 patients with coronary artery disease. Coronary angiograms were taken up to 30 min following intravenous administration of glycosides. The maximum decrease in mean diameters of angiographically normal coronary segments was 10.0 +/- 3.5% (p < or = 0.001) compared to control. Maximum reduction in minimal diameters of stenotic segments was 22.1 +/- 13.1% (p < or = 0.001). This vasoconstriction could be reversed with nitroglycerin. Thus, intravenous administration of digoxin induces vasoconstriction of normal and stenotic coronary arteries, which could cause ischemic complications in the presence of high-grade stenoses. Since digoxin-induced vasoconstriction could be reversed with nitroglycerin, concomitant vasodilator therapy is recommended."},
